{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:2877458 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:2877458 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"2877458","source":"MED","pmid":"2877458","pmcid":"PMC386881","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC386881"]},"doi":"10.1073/pnas.83.21.8132","title":"Sequence analysis of the cDNA encoding human liver glycogen phosphorylase reveals tissue-specific codon usage.","authorString":"Newgard CB, Nakano K, Hwang PK, Fletterick RJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Newgard CB","lastName":"Newgard","firstName":"C B","initials":"CB"},{"fullName":"Nakano K","lastName":"Nakano","firstName":"K","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"Hwang PK","lastName":"Hwang","firstName":"P K","initials":"PK"},{"fullName":"Fletterick RJ","lastName":"Fletterick","firstName":"R J","initials":"RJ"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"21","volume":"83","journalIssueId":793678,"dateOfPublication":"1986 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":1986,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","essn":"1091-6490","issn":"0027-8424","nlmid":"7505876","isoabbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A"},"printPublicationDate":"1986-11-01"},"pubYear":"1986","pageInfo":"8132-8136","abstractText":"We have cloned the cDNA encoding glycogen phosphorylase (1,4-alpha-D-glucan:orthophosphate alpha-D-glucosyl-transferase, EC 2.4.1.1) from human liver. Blot-hybridization analysis using a large fragment of the cDNA to probe mRNA from rabbit brain, muscle, and liver tissues shows preferential hybridization to liver RNA. Determination of the entire nucleotide sequence of the liver message has allowed a comparison with the previously determined rabbit muscle phosphorylase sequence. Despite an amino acid identity of 80%, the two cDNAs exhibit a remarkable divergence in G+C content. In the muscle phosphorylase sequence, 86% of the nucleotides at the third codon position are either deoxyguanosine or deoxycytidine residues, while in the liver homolog the figure is only 60%, resulting in a strikingly different pattern of codon usage throughout most of the sequence. The liver phosphorylase cDNA appears to represent an evolutionary mosaic; the segment encoding the N-terminal 80 amino acids contains greater than 90% G+C at the third codon position. A survey of other published mammalian cDNA sequences reveals that the data for liver and muscle phosphorylases reflects a bias in codon usage patterns in liver and muscle coding sequences in general.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIADDK NIH HHS","acronym":"AM","grantId":"AM 32822","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Muscles","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Liver","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Brain","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Rabbits"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Phosphorylases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"AN","qualifierName":"analysis"}]}}